[[:db/retract 17592186120151 :question/answer-fields 17592186117112] {:db/id 17592186119500 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=5+3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/72/7202d8cbdd95abf0bce85cd66490b29abe5737259def341fcb3202a06c6140a0.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=5+3x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ (0) & =5+3(0) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, ![](images/9b/9b7dd489c2bd902529876d678dd3e21d3503f1d42aafba6aa6b3c40dad8f61da.svg) and ![](images/91/911a47012bbfefca8e19f14c023c3924a5e6e02470d6825ad8e4525816bcf2a2.svg) from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+(-1) \\\\ 5+2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, ![](images/db/dbbce97422b8e84b3408bc64d43a250bcba2bd5f2ba5ea5a6aa282e884fa8750.svg) since substituting the coordinates into the equation $y=5+3x$ gives the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+3x \\\\ 7 & =5+3(-1) \\\\ 7 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119501 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(1,2,2)$ and $(2,1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/cd/cd632a2491479baa770168dc5d143944fa72fe7264f27ee51e5518f9e9b9cbe2.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n{x-1}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n{(0)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(0)-2}{-1}.  \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n![](images/c5/c58eba4b7130df4c9e588d2792fc1cb1f2b465504ae0859edd213735ca690c97.svg)\nHowever, the point $(3,3,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n{(3)-1}\\neq\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}=\\dfrac{(3)-2}{-1}. \\quad {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119502 :question/problem "Let $\\Pi$ be the plane with equation $x+4y-3z=2$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $\\Pi$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $\\Pi$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $\\Pi$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $\\Pi$ is **not** a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/70/70061c1d04780e7a3db2b0c6b41a91c791f029666dff632f35c0ba8d3fd608b0.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The plane does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (0)+4(0)-3(0) & =2 \\\\ 0 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider ![](images/d0/d0cf195a83c36291bae09c62f542449734d024fadae23b99cecbb4507f03c430.svg) and ![](images/13/13f8e15407cd0dbdfc080f19f906df1ed4bf984635cb2eb30b01f95d50695d53.svg) Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+1 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $\\Pi$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4y-3z & =2 \\\\ (3)+4(1)-3(1) & =2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{0} \\not\\in \\Pi,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $\\Pi$ is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186275389 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120150 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $y=1-2x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=1-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-2x \\\\ (0) & =1-2(0) \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1-2x \\\\ (0) & =1-2(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186117106 :answer-field/correct 17592186117108 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120151 :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b" :question/id #uuid "3ea10c69-304c-5ad4-bd04-4affc2cf325b" :question/math-academy-id "q-157376" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(-1,3,1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-t \\\\ 3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 3b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(-a)+(-b) \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ 3a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(-a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $-x=\\frac{y}{3}=z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-32b315b1-b85d-5621-9821-b0256f44b55f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32b315b1-b85d-5621-9821-b0256f44b55f" :answer-field/id #uuid "32b315b1-b85d-5621-9821-b0256f44b55f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-10c070d7-93fe-5bba-a874-0f03d400b970" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10c070d7-93fe-5bba-a874-0f03d400b970" "answer-6f28cf69-6f55-526e-9f18-ac9d9a874611" "answer-6dd9382b-2ca0-59aa-a584-c0b212c9c30e" "answer-525f7819-dd00-5e00-a52d-1b2aea5486bd" "answer-6c2fcb20-55c1-502d-87bf-a84af1affda6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10c070d7-93fe-5bba-a874-0f03d400b970" :answer/id #uuid "10c070d7-93fe-5bba-a874-0f03d400b970" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f28cf69-6f55-526e-9f18-ac9d9a874611" :answer/id #uuid "6f28cf69-6f55-526e-9f18-ac9d9a874611" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dd9382b-2ca0-59aa-a584-c0b212c9c30e" :answer/id #uuid "6dd9382b-2ca0-59aa-a584-c0b212c9c30e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-525f7819-dd00-5e00-a52d-1b2aea5486bd" :answer/id #uuid "525f7819-dd00-5e00-a52d-1b2aea5486bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c2fcb20-55c1-502d-87bf-a84af1affda6" :answer/id #uuid "6c2fcb20-55c1-502d-87bf-a84af1affda6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186275399 :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186376047 :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120047 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
